Q1. What does git diff show?git diff displays the exact line-by-line changes made to your tracked files in the working directory that have not yet been staged for a commit.

Q2. Reading diff output

+ (Plus): Shows lines that were added to the file.

- (Minus): Shows lines that were removed (deleted) from the file.

Q3. Staged vs unstaged changes

Bash
git diff --staged
(Note: git diff --cached also works and does the exact same thing.)

Intermediate
Q4. Compare two specific commits

Bash
git diff HEAD~2 HEAD
(This compares the state from 2 commits ago (HEAD~2) to your current latest commit (HEAD).)

Q5. The diff workflow

Why does git diff show nothing after staging?
By default, git diff only compares your working directory with the staging area. Once you run git add, both match, so there are no unstaged differences left to show.

What command should you run to see the staged changes?git diff --staged

Hard
Q6. Find the mistake in a workflow

Possible reason: The file is a brand-new untracked file (never added with git add before), or the changes in the editor were not saved yet.

Command to verify: git status

What the student should do next: Save the file if unsaved, or run git add <filename> to start tracking the new file (and then use git diff --staged to view it).

Q7. Build a comparison workflow

Before staging, run: git diff report.md

After staging, run: git diff --staged report.md

After committing, run: git diff HEAD~1 HEAD report.md (or git show HEAD report.md)

Submission Tip: Put these answers cleanly into a README.md (or text/markdown file) inside a new public GitHub repository, and paste that repository's URL into the submission box.
